{
  "slug": "verbos-irregulares",
  "number": 9,
  "part": 2,
  "title": "Aller, faire, vouloir, pouvoir",
  "tagline": "Four essential irregular verbs, au and aux, and the future with aller.",
  "goal": "Conjugate the four most useful irregular verbs, say where you're going, ask and invite politely and talk about the future with aller + infinitive.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "conjugacion",
      "title": "The four verbs",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "conjugation",
          "persons": [
            "je",
            "tu",
            "il / elle / on",
            "nous",
            "vous",
            "ils / elles"
          ],
          "verbs": [
            {
              "verb": "aller",
              "meaning": "to go",
              "forms": [
                "je vais",
                "tu vas",
                "il va",
                "nous allons",
                "vous allez",
                "ils vont"
              ]
            },
            {
              "verb": "faire",
              "meaning": "to do, to make",
              "forms": [
                "je fais",
                "tu fais",
                "il fait",
                "nous faisons",
                "vous {faites}",
                "ils font"
              ]
            },
            {
              "verb": "vouloir",
              "meaning": "to want",
              "forms": [
                "je veux",
                "tu veux",
                "il veut",
                "nous voulons",
                "vous voulez",
                "ils veulent"
              ]
            },
            {
              "verb": "pouvoir",
              "meaning": "can, to be able to",
              "forms": [
                "je peux",
                "tu peux",
                "il peut",
                "nous pouvons",
                "vous pouvez",
                "ils peuvent"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "*vous faites*, never «vous faisez». Just like *vous êtes*.",
            "*nous faisons* is pronounced «fuh-zohn»: the *ai* is a short, weak vowel.",
            "*vont, font, sont, ont*: the four most frequent verbs all end the same way with *ils*."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "aller-a",
      "title": "Aller + à: au, à la, à l', aux",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "*aller* is usually followed by *à* (to, at), which merges with the article:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "à + article",
            "Result",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "à + le",
              "*au*",
              "*Je vais {au} cinéma.*"
            ],
            [
              "à + la",
              "*à la*",
              "*Tu vas {à la} plage.*"
            ],
            [
              "à + l'",
              "*à l'*",
              "*Il va {à l'}école.*"
            ],
            [
              "à + les",
              "*aux*",
              "*Nous allons {aux} États-Unis.*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "Just as *de + le = du* [[6]], here *à + le = au*. Never «à le»."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "French uses the article where English doesn't: «I'm going to school» = *Je vais à l'école*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "faire",
      "title": "Faire",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "**Faire + du, de la, de l'** for activities and sports, where English uses «do», «go» or «play»: *faire du sport, faire de la natation* (go swimming). In the negative: *Je ne fais pas de sport.*",
            "**Il fait** for the weather, like «it's» in English: *Il fait froid* (it's cold). Rain and snow have their own verbs: *il pleut, il neige*.",
            "**«Make» and «do» aren't always faire**: to ask a question = *poser une question*; to make a decision = *prendre une décision*."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "vouloir-pouvoir",
      "title": "Vouloir and pouvoir",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "They're conjugated in a very similar way (*veux / peux, veulent / peuvent*) and are usually followed by an **infinitive**, with no «to»:"
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Je veux partir.",
              "tr": "I want to leave."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Tu peux venir ?",
              "tr": "Can you come?"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Nous voulons dîner dehors.",
              "tr": "We want to eat out."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "The polite form",
          "text": "*je veux* (I want) can sound blunt. Use *je voudrais* (I'd like): *Je voudrais un café, s'il vous plaît.* And to ask for something: *Vous pourriez m'aider ?* (Could you help me?)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "futuro",
      "title": "The near future: aller + infinitive",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It works exactly like «going to» in English, and it's the most common way to talk about the future in conversation:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "English",
            "French"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "I'm going to eat.",
              "*Je {vais} manger.*"
            ],
            [
              "You're going to go out.",
              "*Tu {vas} sortir.*"
            ],
            [
              "We're going to travel.",
              "*Nous {allons} voyager.*"
            ],
            [
              "They're going to do sport.",
              "*Ils {vont} faire du sport.*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "There's **no** «to» between the two verbs: «je vais à manger» is wrong → *je vais manger*."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "**Negative:** *ne… pas* goes around *aller*: *Je ne vais pas manger.*"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
